  "worked_solution": "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x$ consists of all values where $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x\\ne 0,$ and is given by the expression\n\n$$\nx\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of $\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}\u2061(\\frac{x}{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{3})-1,$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(\\frac{x}{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{3} & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{5} & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\ \\frac{x}{5} & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & \\ne \\frac{5\\pi }{6}+5n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is $x\\ne \\frac{5\\pi }{6}+5n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
